ype":"Personal","givenName":"S. Z.","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, S. Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X. Z.","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, X. Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y.","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X.","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Zhovkovska","name":"Zhovkovska, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. Z.","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, L. Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X.","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X.","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y.","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Zhukov","name":"Zhukov, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Zuliani","name":"Zuliani, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Zunica","name":"Zunica, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evidence for the decay $B^0_s\\toϕη'$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T10:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:52:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T08:26:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:08:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/jhep10(2026)055","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using a dataset corresponding to an integrated luminosity of $9 \\,\\textrm{fb}^{-1}$ collected in proton-proton collisions between 2011 and 2018 by the LHCb experiment, evidence is found for the decay $B^0_s\\toϕη'$ with $3.5 σ$ significance. The branching ratio relative to the $B^0_s\\toϕϕ$ decay is determined to be $R=(3.56 \\pm 0.79\\pm 0.18\\pm 0.06)\\times10^{-2}$. This corresponds to a branching fraction, $B(B^0_s\\toϕη')=(0.66 \\pm 0.15 \\pm 0.03 \\pm 0.02) \\times 10^{-6}$ where, in both cases, the first uncertainty is statistical, the second systematic, and the third due to external branching fractions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"All figures and tables, along with any supplementary material and additional information, are available at https://lbfence.cern.ch/alcm/public/analysis/full-details/5170"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:23:38Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:23:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Herbert","familyName":"Koch","name":"Koch, Herbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Korteweg-de Vries limit for the global dynamics of the Toda lattice"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Exactly Solvable and Integrable Systems (nlin.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Q53, 37J70, 37K10, 37K60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T10:04:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:50:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T13:40:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:31:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It has been observed that the dynamics of the Toda lattice can be well described by solutions of the Korteweg-de Vries (KdV) equation in the continuum limit. We show that, under the KdV scaling and a suitable translation, the solution of the Toda lattice with H^1 initial data converges to that of the KdV equation globally in time. Our proof relies on tools from harmonic analysis and also on the construction and the conservation of mass and energy of the Toda lattice, the latter of which are derived from the completely integrable structure of the Toda lattice. As a consequence, we obtain long-wave KdV limits for the Toda lattice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages. Minor corrections"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:22:11Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:22:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07402","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07402","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07402","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shulian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shulian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InsHuman: Towards Natural and Identity-Preserving Human Insertion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T07:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:43:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-27T16:19:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:43:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-04T04:32:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:11:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-13T07:56:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:26:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human insertion aims to naturally place specific individuals into a target background. Although existing image editing models may have such ability, they often produce failure cases, including inappropriate human pose in new background, inconsistent number of people, and modified facial identity. Moreover, publicly available human datasets often lack full-body portraits and realistic physical interaction between humans and their background. To address these challenges, we propose InsHuman for natural and identity-preserving human insertion. Specifically, we propose Human-Background Adaptive Fusion (HBAF), which detects foreground humans to obtain a binary mask and applies region-aware weighting to align the human regions between predicted and ground-truth latents, ensuring the person's pose, count, and overall appearance are coherently adapted to the target background.We further propose Face-to-Face ID-Preserving (FFIP), which detects and matches faces between the generated image and the source image in terms of face recognition features to enforce identity consistency for each face.In addition, we propose Bidirectional Data Pairing (BDP) strategy to construct BDP-InsHuman, a high-quality dataset with realistic human-background interactions. Experiments demonstrate that InsHuman achieves significant improvements in generating plausible images while keeping human identity unchanged."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07402","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:19:06Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:19:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tairan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tairan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyu","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Siyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Xiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tools as Continuous Flow for Evolving Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T06:44:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:39:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T11:49:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:40:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-10T02:51:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:26:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in orchestrating tools for reasoning tasks. However, existing methods rely on a step-wise paradigm that lacks a global perspective, which causes error accumulation over long horizons and restricts generalization to unseen tools. To overcome these limitations, we propose Tools as Continuous Flow for Evolving Agentic Reasoning (FlowAgent), which reconceptualizes tool chaining as continuous trajectory generation within a semantic space. To systematically evaluate this paradigm, we introduce the first plan-level closed-loop benchmark dedicated to plan-level agentic reasoning in dynamic real-world environments. Specifically, the proposed FlowAgent leverages conditional flow matching to generate continuous latent trajectories, providing a global planning perspective to ensure coherent and robust tool execution. Theoretically, we establish formal bounds on utility convergence and prove that our continuous formulation fundamentally guarantees robust generalization and error attenuation. Empirical evaluations show that FlowAgent achieves superior robustness and adaptability in long-horizon reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:17:38Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:17:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07322","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07322","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07322","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shagnik","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Shagnik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Sian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ying-Sian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"New results on the odd- and unique-Ramsey numbers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05C55","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T06:28:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:39:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T12:37:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The odd-Ramsey number $r_{\\text{odd}}(n,H)$ of a graph $H$ is the minimum number of colors needed to edge-color $K_n$ so that in every copy of $H$ some color occurs an odd number of times, and the unique-Ramsey number $r_{\\text{u}}(n,H)$ is the corresponding notion in which some color is required to occur not only an odd number of times but exactly once.\n In this paper, we address three questions from previous papers. We show $r_{\\text{odd}}(n,K_{s,t})\u0026gt; n^{1/\\left(\\frac s2+\\frac 1{2\\lfloor t/8 \\rfloor}\\right)}$ when $s\\leq t$ and $s$ is odd and $t$ is even, which is log-asymptotically tight when $s$ is fixed and $t\\to\\infty$. Next, we consider the odd-Ramsey number when the host graph to be edge-colored is a super-Dirac graph, and show that the smallest minimum degree that forces a non-trivial odd-Ramsey number of Hamilton cycles is $n/2+1$. Finally, we show that $r_\\text{u}(n,C_n)\u0026gt; n/4$, which leads to a polynomial gap between $r_\\text{odd}(n,C_n)$ and $r_\\text{u}(n,C_n)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages; improved results on the odd-Ramsey number of Hamilton cycles in super-Dirac graphs"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07322","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:17:14Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:17:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Ostrowska","name":"Ostrowska, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Kukla","name":"Kukla, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriela","familyName":"Majstrak","name":"Majstrak, Gabriela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Opala","name":"Opala, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Pergała","name":"Pergała, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Skwarek","name":"Skwarek, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Wróblewska","name":"Wróblewska, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Surface Learning to Deep Understanding: A Grounded AI Tutoring System for Moodle"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T21:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:42:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.24963/ijcai.2026/984","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This demo paper describes the development of the AI Teaching \\\u0026amp; Learning Assistant, a modular Moodle plugin that leverages Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to deliver high-quality, hallucination-free education. The system employs a dual-centric design, providing students with interactive, Socratic-based tutoring and educators with a \"human-in-the-loop\" workspace for supervised content generation. By grounding Large Language Model (LLM) responses in teacher-provided materials, the assistant addresses the risks of misinformation while encouraging deep conceptual mastery. Evaluation via the Ragas (LLM-as-a-Judge) framework and a preliminary user study confirms its effectiveness, achieving faithfulness scores up to 0.97 and a 4.00/5.00 recommendation rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, accepted as demo paper at IJCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:08:44Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Coleman","name":"Coleman, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouhang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhouhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhankui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhankui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noveen","familyName":"Sachdeva","name":"Sachdeva, Noveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weili","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ed H.","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Ed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivaram","familyName":"Venkataraman","name":"Venkataraman, Shivaram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang-Cheng","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wang-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek Zhiyuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Derek Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beidou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Beidou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PACEvolve++: Improving Test-time Learning for Evolutionary Search Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T23:38:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:22:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T04:10:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:55:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have become drivers of evolutionary search, but most systems rely on a fixed, prompt-elicited policy to sample next candidates. This limits adaptation in practical engineering and research tasks, where evaluations are expensive, and progress depends on learning task-specific search dynamics. We introduce PACEvolve++, an advisor-model reinforcement learning framework for test-time policy adaptation in evolutionary search agents. PACEvolve++ decouples strategic search decisions from implementation: a trainable advisor generates, assesses, and selects hypotheses, while a stronger frontier model translates selected hypotheses into executable candidates. To train the advisor under non-stationary feedback, we propose a phase-adaptive approach that adapts its optimization strategy to different phases of the evolutionary process. Early in evolution, it uses group-relative feedback to learn broad search preferences; later, as reward gaps compress, it emphasizes best-of-$k$ frontier contribution to support stable refinement. Across expert-parallel load balancing, sequential recommendation, and protein fitness extrapolation, PACEvolve++ outperforms the state-of-the-art evolutionary search framework with frontier models, achieving faster convergence and stabilizing test-time training during evolutionary search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:10:31Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:10:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06699","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06699","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06699","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Mensing","name":"Mensing, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Kapar","name":"Kapar, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jochen G.","familyName":"Hirsch","name":"Hirsch, Jochen G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Günther","name":"Günther, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Horst","familyName":"Hahn","name":"Hahn, Horst","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marvin N.","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Marvin N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal synthesis of MRI and tabular data with diffusion in a joint latent space via cross-attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-05T08:30:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T11:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:21:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1117/12.3086603","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a multimodal latent diffusion model that jointly synthesizes volumetric magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and tabular clinical data within a shared latent space via cross-attention. This approach enables coherent joint representation learning of MRI and tabular modalities for generative modeling. Our model utilizes a variational autoencoder to fuse the two modalities before diffusion-based synthesis, allowing modality-appropriate reconstruction with separate decoders for MRI and tabular data. We evaluated the framework on data from the German National Cohort (NAKO Gesundheitsstudie), comprising over 10,000 participants with MRI scans and clinical tabular features such as age, sex, body measurements, and ethnicity. The generated MRI volumes exhibited anatomical plausibility and body composition consistent with the synthesized tabular attributes. Quantitative evaluation using Fréchet distance and precision-recall metrics confirmed high-fidelity image generation. In the tabular modality, our model outperformed CTGAN across standard evaluation metrics and achieved results comparable to TVAE, demonstrating competitive performance relative to established unimodal baselines. This work is, to our knowledge, the first to demonstrate the feasibility of jointly modeling MRI and mixed-type tabular data in a single latent diffusion framework, offering a proof-of-concept for generating coherent synthetic multimodal patient data and aligning with the broader goal of developing digital twins in healthcare."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06699","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:02:26Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:02:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:32:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.08037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanneng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chuanneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristina","familyName":"Klinkner","name":"Klinkner, Kristina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shervin","familyName":"Malmasi","name":"Malmasi, Shervin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Pairs: Your Language Model is Secretly Optimizing a Preference Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T17:26:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:16:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T18:14:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:03:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) aligns language models using pairwise preference comparisons, offering a simple and effective alternative to Reinforcement Learning (RL) from human feedback. However, in many practical settings, training data consists of multiple rollouts per prompt, inducing rich preference structure that pairwise DPO fails to exploit. Collapsing such data into independent pairs discards transitivity, introduces redundant or conflicting supervision, and can lead to unstable optimization. We propose Graph Direct Preference Optimization (GraphDPO), a structured generalization of DPO that operates over directed acyclic preference graphs induced by rollout rankings. GraphDPO encodes dominance relations as edges and optimizes a graph-structured Plackett--Luce-inspired objective that aggregates supervision over dominated neighborhoods, propagating the transitive structure of the input preferences while recovering standard DPO as a special case. GraphDPO naturally handles tied and incomparable responses through an equivalence-class construction: responses with identical preference signals form graph layers, incomparable responses share a layer without an edge, and absent edges contribute zero loss, preventing the spurious gradients that strict total-order objectives incur. Under this layered construction, GraphDPO maintains linear per-prompt complexity via efficient log-sum-exp aggregation. We further incorporate optional ground-truth anchoring by inserting verified solutions as dominant nodes and applying an annealed schedule that stabilizes early training while gradually relaxing oracle supervision. Experiments on reasoning and program synthesis tasks demonstrate superior performance, suggesting that graph-structured preference modeling is a scalable and robust alternative to pairwise and listwise alignment objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:34:08Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:34:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.08022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Udupi","name":"Udupi, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Calvin","familyName":"Ang","name":"Ang, Calvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungyoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sungyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaocong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ChengXiang","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, ChengXiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Convex Optimization of Spiking Neural Networks with Global and Approximation Guarantees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T17:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-26T20:47:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:10:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T03:25:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:26:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spiking neural networks (SNNs) have been proposed as energy-efficient alternatives to deep neural networks. Their training is non-convex and involves a non-differentiable spike function, so existing methods offer no guarantee of reaching a global optimum. We address this issue by formulating SNN optimization as an equivalent convex optimization problem. Specifically, we show that the weight-decay-regularized training problem for a class of threshold RNNs, including leaky integrate-and-fire SNNs, has zero duality gap and the same optimal value as a convex program. Since the resulting convex programs can have exponentially many variables in the amount of data, we introduce a practical randomized surrogate and establish a high-probability guarantee on its approximation quality. Empirically, the best convex variant outperforms surrogate-gradient training on addition and first-last-XOR, matches its performance on sequential MNIST, and trails a reservoir baseline on base-3 and base-5 addition."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:33:47Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:33:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhan","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Jingzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajeeth","familyName":"Vellore","name":"Vellore, Ajeeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajinkya","familyName":"Palwe","name":"Palwe, Ajinkya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahsan","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Ahsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Gorsich","name":"Gorsich, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew P.","familyName":"Castanier","name":"Castanier, Matthew P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"SeungYeon","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, SeungYeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph-Structured Hyperdimensional Computing for Data-Efficient and Explainable Process-Structure-Property Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T16:55:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:04:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multiphoton photoreduction enables high-fidelity fabrication of complex 3D microstructures, yet reliable process-structure-property (PSP) prediction remains difficult because the available data are sparse, heterogeneous, and interaction-dominated. In this regime, conventional feature-vector models are statistically underdetermined, making them prone to spurious correlations, poor regime transfer, and unstable post hoc explanations, whereas mechanistic pipelines depend on calibrated submodels that are rarely available during early process development. We present PSP-HDC, a graph-structured hyperdimensional computing framework that encodes a directed PSP graph as an internal prior for representation, inference, and explanation. A trainable scalar-to-hypervector encoder learns parameter-specific embeddings on a fixed hyperdimensional basis to accommodate heterogeneous scales and noise. Sample representations are then composed through graph-aligned binding and bundling along directed PSP dependencies, and prediction is performed by associative-memory retrieval against class prototypes. Because the same prototype memories support both decision making and attribution, PSP-HDC provides intrinsic explanations at the parameter, group, and within-group levels, while memory alignment and separation quantify prototype formation during training. On sheet-resistance regime prediction for the 3D platform, PSP-HDC achieves an accuracy of 0.910 +/- 0.077 over 1000 random splits and 0.896 under process-fold generalization, outperforming strong baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 18 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:33:16Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:33:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunlei","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Kunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Hutchinson","name":"Hutchinson, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonia","familyName":"Chernova","name":"Chernova, Sonia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Many-to-Many Multi-Agent Pickup and Delivery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T15:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T01:12:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icra57385.2026.11696200","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-robot systems in automated warehouses must manage continuous streams of pickup-and-delivery tasks while ensuring efficiency and safety. Prior work on Multi-Agent Pickup-and-Delivery (MAPD) has largely focused on the one-to-one variant, where each task has a fixed pickup and delivery location. In contrast, real warehouses often present many-to-many MAPD scenarios, where items, tracked by stock keeping unit (SKU) identifiers, can be retrieved from or stored at multiple locations, resulting in an NP-hard four-dimensional assignment problem. To solve the many-to-many MAPD problem, we contribute our algorithm: Many-to-Many Multi-Agent Pickup and Delivery (M2M). We experiment with two variants of our algorithm: one that minimizes estimated task durations (M2M), and one which incorporates SKU distribution into the objective function (M2M-wSKU). Simulation results over 8-hour warehouse operations show that our method consistently matches or outperforms prior state of the art, with M2M completing up to 22,000 more tasks on average across different environments and warehouse inventory densities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:29:29Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:29:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07675","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07675","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07675","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanis","familyName":"Merzouki","name":"Merzouki, Yanis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coral","familyName":"Izquierdo","name":"Izquierdo, Coral","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matei","familyName":"Ignuta-Ciuncanu","name":"Ignuta-Ciuncanu, Matei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcos","familyName":"Gomez-Bracamonte","name":"Gomez-Bracamonte, Marcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Maggioni","name":"Maggioni, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Lombardi","name":"Lombardi, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camilla","familyName":"Mazzoleni","name":"Mazzoleni, Camilla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Martelli","name":"Martelli, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balazs","familyName":"Gunther","name":"Gunther, Balazs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FactoryBench: Evaluating Industrial Machine Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.9","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"cs.AI (primary), cs.RO, cs.LG, cs.CL","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T12:47:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:57:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T21:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:12:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce FactoryBench, a benchmark for evaluating time-series models and LLMs on machine understanding over industrial robotic telemetry. Q\u0026amp;A pairs are organized along four causal levels (state, intervention, counterfactual, decision) instantiating Pearl's ladder of causation, and span five answer formats: four structured formats are scored deterministically and free-form answers are scored by an LLM-as-judge voting protocol. We propose a scalable Q\u0026amp;A generation framework built around structured question templates, present FactoryWave (a dense, multitask, multivariate sensor dataset collected from a UR3 cobot and a KUKA KR10 industrial arm), and construct FactoryBench as a large-scale benchmark of over 70k Q\u0026amp;A items grounded in roughly 15k normalized episodes from FactoryWave, AURSAD, and voraus-AD. Zero-shot evaluation of six frontier LLMs shows that no model exceeds 50% on structured levels or 18% on decision-making, revealing a wide gap between current models and operational machine understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Oral at PUDM, NeurIPS 2026. Code: https://github.com/Forgis-Labs/FactoryBench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07675","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:25:44Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:25:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jichuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jichuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenran","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhenran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanxing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Guanxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuxiong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chuxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changliu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Changliu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BrickCraft: Visuomotor Skill Composition with Situated Manual Guidance for Long-Horizon Interlocking Brick Assembly"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T11:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:54:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T05:25:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:31:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous robotic assembly of interlocking bricks demands seamless integration of long-horizon task reasoning, spatial grounding, and fine-grained manipulation. This paper presents BrickCraft, a compositional framework designed for long-horizon and generalizable interlocking brick assembly. BrickCraft models the assembly process using a relative formulation, where each step is anchored to a reference brick within the partial structure, thereby decomposing complex tasks into a finite set of reusable primitive skills. BrickCraft bridges the gap between high-level assembly plans and physical execution through situated manuals, which provide explicit spatial guidance for learned visuomotor skills by projecting the assembly intent onto real-time robot observations. Finally, BrickCraft employs a modular execution pipeline that chains these spatially grounded skills to accomplish long-horizon assembly tasks. Extensive experimental validations demonstrate that BrickCraft acquires proficient assembly skills from a limited set of demonstrations and effectively composes them for diverse long-horizon assembly tasks. The project website is available at https://intelligent-control-lab.github.io/BrickCraft."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:24:05Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:24:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaike","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Shuaike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Qiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runtian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Runtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiangliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olexandr","familyName":"Isayev","name":"Isayev, Olexandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Agents Price a Reaction? Evaluating LLMs on Chemical Cost Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T05:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:36:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T04:18:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have become increasingly capable as tool-using agents, with benchmarks spanning diverse general agentic tasks. Yet rigorous evaluation of scientific tool use remains limited. In chemistry, recent agents can plan syntheses and invoke domain-specific tools, but evaluations often rely on curated demonstrations, expert assessment, or LLM-as-judge scoring rather than exact, judge-free ground truth. We address this gap with chemical procurement cost estimation, a practical task in which an agent must ground chemical identities, retrieve supplier quotes, select valid purchasable packs, normalize quantities, and compute cost from a reaction description. We introduce ChemCost, a benchmark of 1,427 evaluable reactions grounded to a frozen pricing snapshot covering 2,261 chemicals and 230,775 supplier quotes, supporting scalar scoring and stage-level diagnosis of grounding, retrieval, procurement, and arithmetic failures. To evaluate robustness, we further construct controlled noise-injected views that perturb chemical aliases, quantity expressions, missing fields, and input formatting. Experiments with frontier, open-weight, and chemistry-specialized LLM agents show that tool access is necessary but insufficient for solving the task. The strongest agents reach only 50.6% accuracy within 25% relative error on clean inputs and degrade substantially with realistic noise. Stage-level analysis further shows that failures arise from brittle parsing, ineffective evidence integration, invalid pack selection, and non-convergent tool use."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:15:34Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:15:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasileios P.","familyName":"Kemerlis","name":"Kemerlis, Vasileios P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Ateniese","name":"Ateniese, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgenios M.","familyName":"Kornaropoulos","name":"Kornaropoulos, Evgenios M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TENNOR: Trustworthy Execution for Neural Networks through Obliviousness and Retrievals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T02:46:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:30:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T03:47:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:24:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T20:33:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3830454.3846750","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training neural networks on sensitive data in untrusted cloud environments requires simultaneously achieving computational efficiency and rigorous privacy guarantees. Sparsification techniques, essential for scalable training of wide layers, expose input-dependent memory-access patterns that are visible and can be exploited by a host OS/hypervisor, even when computation is protected by a Trusted Execution Environment. We present TENNOR, a system that resolves this tension by co-designing the neural network training pipeline with doubly oblivious primitives, eliminating value-dependent neural network access-pattern leakage while also utilizing adaptive sparsification. TENNOR recasts sparse neuron activation as a locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) retrieval problem, reducing secure sparsification to doubly oblivious accesses over an LSH data structure. To eliminate the prohibitive storage cost of \"multi-table\" LSH, we introduce Multi-Probe Winner-Take-All (MP-WTA): the first multi-probe scheme for rank-based LSH, achieving a $50\\times$ reduction in (hash table) memory while preserving model accuracy. We evaluate TENNOR on multi-label classification benchmarks, with output layers of up to hundreds of thousands of neurons under different batch sizes inside an Intel TDX Trusted Domain, achieving per-batch training speedups of $13\\times$-$470\\times$ over a Path ORAM baseline and reducing a 208-hour run to about 26 minutes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages. Accepted at ACM CCS 2026. Updated to reflect the camera-ready version, with additional material and appendices"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:13:23Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:13:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Friedman","name":"Friedman, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Donghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Fifty","name":"Fifty, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jang-Hyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jang-Hyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhi","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek","familyName":"Rathod","name":"Rathod, Vivek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhuwan","familyName":"Dhingra","name":"Dhingra, Bhuwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LensVLM: Selective Context Expansion for Compressed Visual Representation of Text"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T23:03:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T04:51:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:43:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T04:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:30:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-09T10:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:52:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Models (VLMs) offer the exciting possibility of processing text as rendered images, bypassing the need for tokenizing the text into long token sequences. Since VLM image encoders map fixed-size images to a fixed number of visual tokens, varying rendering resolution provides a fine-grained compression knob. However, accuracy deteriorates quickly as compression increases: characters shrink below the vision encoder's effective resolution, making them indistinguishable. To address this, we propose LensVLM, an inference framework and post-training recipe that enables VLMs to scan compressed images, then selectively expand only the relevant images to their uncompressed form via learned tools. Building on Qwen3.5-9B-Base, LensVLM maintains accuracy comparable to the full-text upper bound at 4.3$\\times$ effective compression and outperforms retrieval-based, text- and visual-compression baselines up to 10.1$\\times$ effective compression across seven text QA benchmarks. LensVLM also generalizes to multimodal document and code understanding tasks, with the accuracy gain over baselines growing as compression increases. Our analysis validates this approach: training makes visual compression robust to rendering choices, and as compression grows the model increasingly relies on expanded content rather than unreliable visual reading. The analysis also yields practical tool-choice guidance: text expansion is preferable for rendered text, while high-resolution image expansion suits native documents whose layout cues carry task-relevant information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:10:03Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:10:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivaram","familyName":"Subramanian","name":"Subramanian, Shivaram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Ettl","name":"Ettl, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingdong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayant","familyName":"Kalagnanam","name":"Kalagnanam, Jayant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constrained Triple-Head Price Optimization (C3PO) Network for Discrete-Choice Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T20:55:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T18:22:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:04:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a Constrained Triple-Head Price Optimization (C3PO) network for real-time bi-level decision making in discrete-choice environments. In these settings, a seller selects product or service offerings to achieve business objectives, while heterogeneous users make personalized acceptance or rejection decisions based on their preferences, ultimately determining seller outcomes. Existing approaches that fine-tune pre-trained tabular foundation models on synthetic data are limited by their imitation-learning paradigm and inability to enforce common pricing rules, including cross-product and global constraints. C3PO addresses these limitations through a novel transformer-based architecture that jointly integrates price imitation learning, multi-task revenue prediction, and in-context elasticity learning to generate robust pricing recommendations across domains without retraining. A lightweight differentiable constraint layer enables generated prices to satisfy business constraints while optimizing the revenue objective. We demonstrate strong out-of-distribution performance on simulated, synthetic, and real-world datasets. Across simulated benchmarks where the calibrated MNL model is misspecified relative to the ground-truth choice model, C3PO improves expected revenue by 1.4 to 7.9 percentage points over a calibrated MNL baseline. The constraint layer reduces average absolute constraint violations by more than 50\\%. We further deploy the model in real-world applications, including healthcare tender pricing and airline ancillary pricing, where C3PO improves directional pricing effectiveness, measured by price-increase and price-decrease recall, by 18--34 percentage points depending on the baselines considered, including calibrated MNL, classical optimization, and off-the-shelf tabular foundation-models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:08:13Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:08:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismail","familyName":"Alkhouri","name":"Alkhouri, Ismail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng-Han","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Cheng-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvaro","familyName":"Velasquez","name":"Velasquez, Alvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susmit","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Susmit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongrong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rongrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentiable Repairability \u0026amp; Mutations for Scalable Combinatorial Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T20:32:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:12:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:46:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:18:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Combinatorial optimization problems such as Maximum Independent Set (MIS) and Maximum Cut (MaxCut) arise across a wide range of applications, yet solving large-scale instances remains computationally challenging. While discrete local-search procedures can produce high-quality solutions, their inherently sequential and iterative nature can become increasingly expensive as problem size and density grow. In this paper, we present a differentiable perspective on combinatorial optimization that leverages continuous relaxation with parallelizable gradient-based updates to improve scalability while reducing dependence on discrete local search. More specifically, for MIS, we show that solutions that remain repairable through a discrete (1,2)-swap can nevertheless be fixed points of projected gradient ascent, revealing a fundamental limitation of purely local gradient dynamics. For MaxCut, we develop a new quadratic formulation whose landscape provably allows projected gradient ascent to escape 1-flip-repairable solutions while ensuring binary local maximizers. Building on these insights, and inspired by discrete perturb-and-improve methods, we introduce a framework that combines projected gradient ascent with differentiable mutations (partial resets) to efficiently explore new regions of the solution space}. Experimental results on MIS and MaxCut demonstrate the potential of this approach for scalable combinatorial optimization, particularly in regimes where conventional discrete local search becomes computationally prohibitive."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:07:45Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:07:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:52:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LPSM"],"givenName":"Lokman A","familyName":"Abbas-Turki","name":"Abbas-Turki, Lokman A","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ENSAE Paris"],"givenName":"Jean-François","familyName":"Chassagneux","name":"Chassagneux, Jean-François","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LPSM"],"givenName":"Jean-Philippe","familyName":"Lemor","name":"Lemor, Jean-Philippe","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LPSM"],"givenName":"Grégoire","familyName":"Loeper","name":"Loeper, Grégoire","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LPSM"],"givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Sananes","name":"Sananes, Simon","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic Policy Gradient Methods in the Uncertain Volatility Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Pricing of Securities (q-fin.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-01T08:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T15:18:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:36:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T06:21:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:33:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The multidimensional Uncertain Volatility Model leads to robust option pricing problems under joint volatility and correlation uncertainty. Their numerical resolution quickly becomes challenging because the associated stochastic control problem is high-dimensional. We propose a backward actor-critic stochastic policy-gradient scheme tailored to this setting. The method combines a discrete dynamic programming principle with Proximal Policy Optimization and one-hidden-layer neural-network approximations of both the value function and the control policy. A key ingredient is the policy parameterization: continuous controls are represented through a squashed Gaussian policy built on a $C$-vine representation of correlation matrices, which enforces positive definiteness by construction. Beyond the robust price itself, the spatial gradient of the trained critic provides an approximation of the associated superhedging strategy. We assess the quality of this learned gradient through a dual formulation, which also yields a numerical dual estimate of the price. Numerical experiments on a range of multidimensional derivatives show that the method yields accurate prices, remains computationally efficient, and compares favorably with existing Monte Carlo and machine-learning-based benchmarks for robust pricing in the Uncertain Volatility Model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:01:45Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:01:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:51:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.08036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mads Greisen","familyName":"Højlund","name":"Højlund, Mads Greisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"August Smart","familyName":"Lykke-Møller","name":"Lykke-Møller, August Smart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Moss","name":"Moss, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ove","familyName":"Christiansen","name":"Christiansen, Ove","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Get Your Kroneckers in a Twist: Gaussian Processes on High-Dimensional Incomplete Grids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T17:24:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:16:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T18:46:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:07:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce CUTS-GPR, a new method for performing numerically exact GPR in high-dimensional settings. The key component of CUTS-GPR is an extremely fast kernel matrix-vector product, which exhibits near-linear or even linear scaling with the amount of training data, $N$, and low-order polynomial scaling with dimensionality, $D$. This is obtained by combining an additive kernel with an incomplete grid and exploiting the resulting structure of the kernel matrix. The scalability of the matrix-vector product is verified by benchmarks with billions of data points and thousands of dimensions. We demonstrate the end-to-end scalability of CUTS-GPR by running full GPR calculations, including hyperparameter optimization, on synthetic datasets with up to $N = 4\\,494\\,001$ and $D = 500$. As a realistic and challenging test, we finally apply CUTS-GPR to a set of ten potential energy surfaces (PESs) with $N = 447\\,265$ and $D = 24$. The calculations are completed in a matter of hours, showing that CUTS-GPR enables Bayesian modelling of high-dimensional PESs - a longstanding challenge in computational chemistry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"63 pages, 21 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:34:07Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:34:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07808","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07808","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07808","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamil","familyName":"Ciosek","name":"Ciosek, Kamil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Banafsheh","familyName":"Rafiee","name":"Rafiee, Banafsheh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Ghiassian","name":"Ghiassian, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolò","familyName":"Felicioni","name":"Felicioni, Nicolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Minimax Rate of Perturbed Second-Order Calibration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T14:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:04:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T15:36:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:22:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Second-order calibration error quantifies how closely a higher-order predictor's epistemic-uncertainty estimate matches the conditional variance of the label probability on its level sets. We characterize the minimax rate of estimating the second-order calibration error for binary classification in the regime where a small perturbation is applied to the classifier outputs. Our procedure is simple: add independent bandwidth-$h$ sech noise to the score coordinates, then regress $Y^{(1)}$ and $Y^{(1)}Y^{(2)}$ on the perturbed score using low-degree polynomials. Crucially, the sech perturbation makes the calibration functions analytic in a suitable strip. The resulting estimator has error $O_h(\\log^{3/2}n/\\sqrt n)$, with explicit constants. In the same setting, a matching $Ω(1/\\sqrt{n})$ lower bound establishes minimax optimality up to logarithmic factors. As a corollary, we give a finite-sample guarantee for perturbed second-order Platt scaling, yielding a post-hoc procedure that recalibrates both the mean prediction and the epistemic-variance estimate of the perturbed higher-order predictor. Along the way, we give an explicit two-moment formulation of second-order calibration error and relate it quantitatively to the bucketed formulation of Ahdritz et al. [2025]. Our experiments confirm the predicted rate and the quality of the recalibrated uncertainties."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07808","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:28:52Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:28:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yebin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yebin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvia","familyName":"Zuffi","name":"Zuffi, Silvia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Goetz","name":"Goetz, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAM 3D Animal: Promptable Animal 3D Reconstruction from Images in the Wild"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T11:26:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:54:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T12:53:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D animal reconstruction in the wild remains challenging due to large species variation, frequent occlusions, and the prevalence of multi-animal scenes, while existing methods predominantly focus on single-animal settings. We present SAM 3D Animal, the first promptable framework for multi-animal 3D reconstruction from a single image. Built on the SMAL+ parametric animal model, our method jointly reconstructs multiple instances and supports flexible prompts in the form of keypoints and masks which enable more reliable disambiguation in crowded and occluded scenes. To train such a model, we further introduce Herd3D, a multi-animal 3D dataset containing over 5K images, designed to increase diversity in species, interactions, and occlusion patterns. Experiments on the Animal3D, APTv2, and Animal Kingdom datasets show that our framework achieves state-of-the-art results over both existing model-based and model-free methods, demonstrating a scalable and effective solution for prompt-driven animal 3D reconstruction in the wild."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2026 Oral. Project website: https://georgehux.com/SAM3D-Animal-project-page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:24:03Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:24:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Knopf","name":"Knopf, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Waid","name":"Waid, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Gundacker","name":"Gundacker, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Onder","name":"Onder, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Radmanovac","name":"Radmanovac, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Gaggl","name":"Gaggl, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulio","familyName":"Bordieri","name":"Bordieri, Giulio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Cordoni","name":"Cordoni, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta","familyName":"Missiaggia","name":"Missiaggia, Marta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Verroi","name":"Verroi, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulio","familyName":"Magrin","name":"Magrin, Giulio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Bergauer","name":"Bergauer, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Hirtl","name":"Hirtl, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measurements of the micro-spill structure of medical cyclotron and synchrotron beams and its impact on pulse pileup"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T09:40:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:48:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T07:23:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:02:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1748-0221/21/10/p10009","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detector characterization and instrumentation testing are often performed at cyclotron and synchrotron facilities, many of which were originally developed for medical applications in cancer therapy. For particle physics experiments requiring a single-particle resolution, pileup can significantly degrade data quality, making precise knowledge of the beam time structure essential for selecting appropriate readout parameters. However, such information is often unavailable from the facilities and challenging to determine experimentally. Here, we report measurements of the spill time structure at two medical accelerator facilities using a silicon carbide (SiC) particle sensor coupled to a high-frequency readout system. Owing to its high carrier saturation velocity and the tolerance to large bias voltages, SiC is well suited for fast readout and measurements requiring precise timing. Using a 6 GHz readout with custom SiC diodes, we characterize the micro-spill structure of both cyclotron and synchrotron beams on a sub-nanosecond timescale. The measured arrival-time distributions exhibit modulation with the accelerator RF frequencies, reflecting features of the extraction process. The resolved micro-spill structure enables quantitative estimation of pileup contributions and provides design constraints for future readout electronics. The presented results emphasize the importance of the characterization of the beam time-structure characterization for the development of precise readout systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Revised submission to JINST after minor reviews"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:21:37Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:21:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghai","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Qinghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangbin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Guangbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Sabadini","name":"Sabadini, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenghua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhenghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Octonionic Riesz-Dunford functional calculus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 17A35, Secondary 46S10, 47B37, 47A70","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T03:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:31:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T09:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:53:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Riesz-Dunford functional calculus over the algebra of octonions, denoted by $\\mathbb{O}$, has long been an open problem due to the nonassociativity of octonions. Two core obstacles hinder its development: first, the generalization of the resolvent operator series identity produces unexpected associator terms that invalidate standard expansions; second, the nonassociativity spoils the analyticity of the resolvent operator, a key property for defining a functional calculus via Cauchy integrals. In this paper, we initiate the study of the Riesz-Dunford functional calculus for bounded power-associative para-linear operators in Banach octonionic bimodules. To address the above issues, we introduce several pivotal concepts: power-associative operators (to eliminate the unwanted associator terms and recover valid resolvent series expansions), the notions of regular inverse of $R_s-T$ for $s\\in Ø$ (which serve as the octonionic versions of the resolvent operator), $\\mathbb{C}_J$-extendable power-associative operators, and $\\mathbb{C}_J$-liftable power-associative operators (to characterize the slice regularity of the resolvent operators). Based on these notions, we define two types of octonionic spectra: the pull-back spectrum $σ^*(T)$ and the push-forward spectrum $σ_*(T)$. These give rise to the left and right slice regular functional calculi of bounded power-associative para-linear operators, respectively.\n This theory unifies the Riesz-Dunford functional calculus over division algebras ($ \\mathbb{C}, \\mathbb{H}, \\mathbb{O}$) and fills the six-decade-long gap in octonionic (nonassociative) functional analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"69pages,3figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:13:58Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:13:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zicheng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liping","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Liping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaohua","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiteGUI: Lightweight GUI Agents via Multi-Solution Guided Distillation and Dual-Level Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T09:38:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T08:52:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:49:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present LiteGUI, a new framework for building lightweight GUI agents. GUI interaction poses unique challenges due to the long-horizon nature of complex tasks and the existence of multiple valid interaction paths, which are difficult for lightweight GUI agents to handle effectively. To address these challenges, LiteGUI introduces a two-stage post-training framework operating on logged GUI states. First, we propose Guided On-Policy Distillation, which provides training-time privileged teacher guidance at each GUI state by selecting the most-matched valid action from human-verified multi-solution action annotations, while leaving the student's on-policy rollout unchanged. Second, we develop Multi-Solution Dual-Level GRPO, which combines action-level supervision with history-conditioned, per-state planning-quality supervision while accounting for multiple valid actions at each logged GUI state. Together, these stages support multi-step GUI tasks without requiring the student to reproduce fixed demonstration trajectories. We further develop a scalable data generation pipeline and a corresponding multi-solution dataset, Lite-Dataset, to support the training and evaluation of GUI agents, addressing a gap in the existing literature. Extensive experiments across multiple GUI benchmarks demonstrate that LiteGUI substantially improves the performance of lightweight GUI agents over existing training paradigms, including SFT, on-policy distillation and GRPO, and achieves competitive or superior performance compared with substantially larger models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:21:33Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:21:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengju","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pengju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiwen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Caiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the Last Mile of Circuit Design: PostEDA-Bench, a Hierarchical Benchmark for PPA Convergence and DRC Fixing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T20:54:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:13:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T20:26:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:07:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T15:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-07T22:33:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:18:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are increasingly applied to the \"last mile\" of Electronic Design Automation (EDA): repairing residual sign-off Design Rule Check (DRC) violations and converging Power-Performance-Area (PPA) targets after tool runs. Existing EDA-LLM benchmarks, however, omit DRC fixing entirely and rely on flat hierarchies tied to a single toolchain. We introduce PostEDA-Bench, a hierarchical benchmark with 145 tasks across DRC-Essential, DRC-Reasoning, PPA-Mono, and PPA-Multi, supported by EDA toolchains with machine-checkable evaluation. Across eight commercial and open-source LLMs under multiple agent scaffolds, we find that agents handle synthetic DRC-Essential and single-objective PPA-Mono reasonably well but degrade sharply on the more practical DRC-Reasoning, where the best success rate is 36.66%, and PPA-Multi, where the best success rate is 20.00%; vision augmentation consistently enhances DRC-Bench; and trade-off reasoning, rather than knob knowledge, is the dominant PPA-Multi bottleneck."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:08:06Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:08:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giacomo","familyName":"Spigler","name":"Spigler, Giacomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAVIS: A Benchmark for Egocentric Active Vision and Anticipatory Gaze in Imitation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T16:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:12:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T18:52:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:07:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Active vision -- where a policy controls its own gaze during manipulation -- has emerged as a key capability for imitation learning, with multiple independent systems demonstrating its benefits in the past year. Yet there is no shared benchmark to compare approaches or quantify what active vision contributes, on which task types, and under what conditions. We introduce TAVIS, evaluation infrastructure for active-vision imitation learning, with two complementary task suites -- TAVIS-Head (5 tasks, global search via pan/tilt necks) and TAVIS-Hands (3 tasks, local occlusion via wrist cameras) -- on two humanoid torso embodiments (GR1T2, Reachy2), built on IsaacLab. TAVIS provides three evaluation primitives: a paired headcam-vs-fixedcam protocol on identical demonstrations; GALT (Gaze-Action Lead Time), a novel metric grounded in cognitive science and HRI that quantifies anticipatory gaze in learned policies; and procedural ID/OOD splits. Baseline experiments with Diffusion Policy and $π_0$ reveal that (i) active vision generally helps, consistently across training runs, but benefits are task-conditional rather than uniform; (ii) multi-task policies degrade sharply under controlled distribution shifts on both suites; and (iii) imitation alone yields anticipatory gaze that lands on the task-relevant object, with lead times comparable to those of the human demonstrations, although head motion is less smooth than in the demonstrations, an effect of action chunking that success rate does not reveal. Code and evaluation scripts are released at https://github.com/spiglerg/tavis; demonstrations (LeRobot v3.0; ~2200 episodes) and trained baselines at https://huggingface.co/tavis-benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 6 figures, 14 tables. v2: revised and extended (inter-seed variability, wrist-camera ablation, GALT validation and sensitivity analyses, gaze behaviour statistics). Project page: https://tavis-bench.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:31:59Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:32:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:07:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07922","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07922","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07922","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tue M.","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Tue M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang X.","familyName":"Nhat","name":"Nhat, Hoang X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raed","familyName":"Alharbi","name":"Alharbi, Raed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phi Le","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Phi Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"My T.","familyName":"Thai","name":"Thai, My T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tree SAE: Learning Hierarchical Feature Structures in Sparse Autoencoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T15:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:10:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T02:18:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:35:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T03:29:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:30:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning hierarchical features in Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) is essential for capturing the structured nature of real-world data and mitigating issues like feature absorption or splitting. Existing works attempt to identify hierarchical relationships within independent feature sets by relying on activation coverage, the assumption that child feature should only activate when its parent feature activates. However, we demonstrate that this condition alone is insufficient; that is, it often produces false positives where parent and child concepts are semantically unrelated. To address this, we introduce a novel reconstruction condition that enforces a deeper functional link between hierarchical levels. By combining both activation and reconstruction constraints, we propose the Tree SAE, a model designed to learn hierarchical structures directly from within the feature set. Our results demonstrate that Tree SAEs significantly surpass the existing SAEs at learning hierarchical pairs while maintaining competitive performance to the state-of-the-art on several key benchmarks. Finally, we demonstrate the practical utility of our Tree SAE in mapping the geometry of child feature subspaces and uncovering the complex hierarchical concept structures encoded within large language models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07922","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:31:30Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:31:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:07:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07803","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07803","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07803","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuncheng","familyName":"You","name":"You, Yuncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global Dynamics and Synchronization of Hodgkin-Huxley-Wilson Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"34D06, 34A08, 37N25, 92B20, 92C20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T14:37:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:04:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-12T12:56:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:47:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T22:37:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:18:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hodgkin-Huxley equations as a monumental breakthrough in neuroscience theory had been distilled into many simplified models to study, but this mathematical model itself was not fully investigated in terms of global and asymptotic dynamics due to its strong nonlinearity and higher dimensionality. In this paper a new model called Hodgkin-Huxley-Wilson neural networks is proposed and investigated. This model captures the essential features of the nonlinearity and the conductances of two dominant ionic current channels coupled with the neuron membrane voltage in gated firing and bursting functionality of biological neural networks in the original Hodgkin-Huxley model. Through the uniform and sharp \\emph{a priori} estimates purely by mathematical hard analysis on the solutions of the model equations and then the interneuron differencing equations of gap functions, it is rigorously proved that global solution dynamics are robustly dissipative with a sharp ultimate bound and that exponential synchronization of the Hodgkin-Huxley-Wilson neural networks at a uniform convergence rate occurs if the interneuron coupling strength satisfies an explicitly computable threshold condition. The synchronization result with fractional-power convergence rate is also proved for time-fractional memristive Hodgkin-Huxley-Wilson neural networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07803","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:28:45Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:28:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:07:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06799","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06799","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06799","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madeleine","familyName":"Ginolin","name":"Ginolin, Madeleine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Grayling","name":"Grayling, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaisey S.","familyName":"Mandel","name":"Mandel, Kaisey S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Autenrieth","name":"Autenrieth, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin M.","familyName":"Boyd","name":"Boyd, Benjamin M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Do","name":"Do, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Kelsey","name":"Kelsey, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"O'Callaghan","name":"O'Callaghan, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the origin of the environmental dependence of SN Ia magnitudes: A BayeSN view of the ZTF SN Ia DR2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T18:01:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:04:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T09:40:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:57:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-17T12:11:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-07T13:37:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag1785","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Astrophysical variabilities of Type Ia supernovae (SNe Ia), such as their link with their birth environment, are now one of the leading sources of systematic uncertainties on the measurement of the dark energy equation-of-state parameter $w$. Population studies of SNe Ia, using large samples, give precious insights into these variabilities. We analyse a volume-limited subsample of 932 SNe from the ZTF SN Ia DR2 with BayeSN, a hierarchical Bayesian model for SN Ia SEDs. We investigate the distributions of SN Ia light curve parameters and their link with SN environment. Using a new training of BayeSN released in a companion paper, we find a smaller scatter of Hubble residuals compared to SALT. We then investigate the magnitude step, which accounts for the correlation between SN Ia standardised absolute magnitude and host environments. We find a posteriori steps of $0.103\\pm0.010$ mag (a $10.1σ$ difference from 0) when using global stellar mass as an environmental proxy, and $0.086\\pm0.010$ mag ($8.3σ$) when using local colour, in accordance with steps computed using SALT light curve fits. This confirms that the large step seen in the ZTF SN Ia DR2 data was not due to the SALT fit or the associated standardisation process. We then investigate the origin of the step, using a BayeSN model which accounts for both an intrinsic magnitude step and differing dust properties with the SN environment. We find a $0.103\\pm0.018$ mag ($5.6σ$) step in global mass and a $0.085\\pm0.019$ mag ($4.5σ$) step in local colour. The means of the $R_V$ distribution are similar between different host environments, with $Δ\\mathbb{E}(R_V)\\leq0.2$ across all environment proxies, with significances ranging from $0.6σ$ to $1.2σ$. This is a strong signal of the existence of an intrinsic dependence of SN Ia absolute magnitude on environment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in MNRAS. 14 pages, 13 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06799","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:04:51Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:04:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:07:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesar","familyName":"Ayala","name":"Ayala, Cesar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gorazd","familyName":"Cvetic","name":"Cvetic, Gorazd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Renormalon-based resummation for B(D) mesons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T22:20:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:18:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T10:16:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:52:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We apply a previously developed method of renormalon-based resummation of spacelike and timelike QCD observables, to evaluate of the values of the pole masses $m_q$ of $q=b$ and $c$ quarks, using as input the knowledge of the values of the corresponding ${\\overline{\\rm MS}}$ masses ${\\overline m}_q \\equiv {\\overline m}_q({\\overline m}_q^2)$. The evaluation also uses the knowledge of the first few coefficients of the perturbation expansion of $m_q$ (i.e., of $m_q/{\\overline m}_q$), as well as the known renormalon structure of that expansion. In the evaluation, we use the timelike QCD running coupling based on a specific holomorphic spacelike QCD coupling, in order to avoid additional ambiguities due to the Landau poles of the usual perturbative coupling. The principal IR regulator parameter of the coupling is varied in an expected range. We also reevaluate the chromomagnetic Wilson coefficient of heavy quark ${\\hat C}(m_q^2)$, and extract values of several corresponding hadronic parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures; v2: extended discussion of uncertainties; references added; to appear in EPJC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:09:31Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:09:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:07:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. Sagar","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, S. Sagar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Lal","name":"Lal, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Tiedau","name":"Tiedau, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. V.","familyName":"Okhapkin","name":"Okhapkin, M. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Peik","name":"Peik, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$J=0$ metastable state of $\\mathrm{Th}^{2+}$ for a hyperfine-free nuclear clock"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atomic Physics (physics.atom-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T14:36:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:46:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/c98g-6f84","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present measurements on a metastable state in $\\mathrm{Th}^{2+}$ with the electronic configuration $6d^2\\,{}^3P_0\\,(5090\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$. This is motivated by the prospect of using the state in laser excitation of the low-energy $^{229}$Th nuclear resonance independent from the leading hyperfine interactions. The $6d^2\\,{}^3P_0$ state has no dipole-allowed radiative decay channel and is connected to the second ground state $6d^2\\,{}^3F_2\\,(63\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ through an electric quadrupole transition only. We populate the state by laser excitation at 484~nm via a higher excited level and detect its population in laser-induced fluorescence. The isotope shift of the $J=0$ level between $^{232}\\mathrm{Th}^{2+}$ and $^{229}\\mathrm{Th}^{2+}$ is determined as a measure of the interaction of electronic and nuclear charge distributions. The lifetime of the level in our ion trap with buffer gas is limited by collisional mixing with the nearby state $5f6d\\,{}^3G_3\\,(5060\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$. In ultrahigh vacuum, it could serve as a hyperfine-free nuclear clock that is largely immune to field-induced frequency shifts via the electron shell."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:28:44Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:28:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:20:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07576","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07576","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07576","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rémi","familyName":"Abgrall","name":"Abgrall, Rémi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Dumbser","name":"Dumbser, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre-Henri","familyName":"Maire","name":"Maire, Pierre-Henri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Zampa","name":"Zampa, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On structure-preserving and pointwise conservative continuous DG schemes for hyperbolic systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T10:46:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:52:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T07:49:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a new class of structure-preserving semi-discrete continuous-discontinuous Galerkin (CG-DG) finite element schemes for linear and nonlinear hyperbolic systems of partial differential equations on unstructured simplex meshes that automatically satisfy the following properties: i) the new schemes are not only cellwise conservative, but also locally pointwise conservative everywhere, hence they satisfy the integral form of the conservation law on arbitrary control volumes that do not have to coincide with the mesh at all; ii) the new methods naturally satisfy the two basic vector calculus identities $\\nabla \\cdot \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{A}$ and $\\nabla \\times \\nabla Z$ exactly pointwise locally and globally everywhere on the discrete level; iii) for linear symmetric hyperbolic systems the schemes are naturally energy conservative for the square energy, i.e. nonlinearly stable in the $L^2$ norm. The key ingredient of the new CG-DG schemes is the use of two different but compatible approximation spaces: the classical DG space $\\mathcal{U}_h^N$ of discontinuous piecewise polynomials of degree up to $N$ and a classical finite element space $\\mathcal{W}_h^{N+1}$ of globally continuous piecewise polynomials of degree $N+1$. In the new CG-DG schemes, the discrete solution $\\mathbf{u}_h$ is sought in $\\mathcal{U}_h^N$, while a suitable discrete flux field $\\tilde{\\mathbf{f}}_h$ is computed in $\\mathcal{W}_h^{N+1}$. For $N=0$ our new schemes are directly related to cell-centered finite volume schemes with suitable vertex-based fluxes. All claimed properties of the schemes are first mathematically proven and are then also verified via suitable numerical tests. We show applications of our approach to three linear and nonlinear hyperbolic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07576","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:23:15Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:23:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:20:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhengtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutian","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yutian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yeping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"She","name":"She, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdeslam","familyName":"Boularias","name":"Boularias, Abdeslam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Visual Feature-Based World Models via Residual Latent Action"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T00:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T20:17:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models predict future transitions from observations and actions. Existing works predominantly focus on image generation only. Visual feature-based world models, on the other hand, predict future visual features instead of raw video pixels, offering a promising alternative that is more efficient and less prone to hallucination. However, current feature-based approaches rely on direct regression, which leads to blurry or collapsed predictions in complex interactions, while generative modeling in high-dimensional feature spaces still remains challenging. In this work, we discover that a new type of latent action representation, which we refer to as Residual Latent Action (RLA), can be easily learned from DINO residuals. We also show that RLA is predictive, generalizable, and encodes temporal progression. Building on RLA, we propose RLA World Model (RLA-WM), which predicts RLA values via flow matching. RLA-WM outperforms both state-of-the-art feature-based and video-diffusion world models on simulation and real-world datasets, while being orders of magnitude faster than video diffusion. Furthermore, we develop two robot learning techniques that use RLA-WM to improve policy learning. The first one is a minimalist world action model with RLA that learns from actionless videos, and improves VLA on LIBERO and real robot. The second one is a visual RL framework trained entirely inside a world model learned from offline videos only, using a video-aligned reward and no online interactions. Project page: https://mlzxy.github.io/rla-wm"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:11:27Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:11:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:20:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayato","familyName":"Takeshita","name":"Takeshita, Hayato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuaki","familyName":"Kosako","name":"Kosako, Kazuaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norikazu","familyName":"Kinoshita","name":"Kinoshita, Norikazu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukinobu","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Yukinobu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Analysis of the energy and angular distributions of photoneutrons from natPb, 197Au, natSn, natCu, natFe, and natTi using resonance direct theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T00:35:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T06:18:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/bjrh-h9kg","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Photoneutron double-differential cross sections in the giant dipole resonance (GDR) region were calculated to investigate the underlying nuclear reaction mechanisms, with particular emphasis on the role of the direct process. Contributions from direct, pre-equilibrium, and compound processes were all taken into account. Wilkinson's resonance direct (RD) theory, based on the independent particle model, was applied to describe high-energy neutron emission from the direct process. The angular distribution of neutrons emitted via the RD mechanism was formulated using the Agodi and Courant formalism, which was incorporated into the RD framework. Neutron emission from the pre-equilibrium and compound processes was calculated using the two-component exciton model and the Hauser-Feshbach formalism, respectively. The calculated results were compared with experimental data obtained at NewSUBARU using 16.6-MeV quasi-monochromatic linearly-polarized photon beams. Good agreement between calculations and measurements was observed for Pb, Au, and Sn, confirming the validity of the proposed model. Furthermore, the angular anisotropies of photoneutrons emitted from these elements were investigated, revealing considerable contributions from the RD process at high neutron energies. This study provides a deeper understanding of photoneutron emission mechanisms in the GDR energy region."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:11:14Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:11:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:20:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06866","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06866","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06866","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ege C.","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Ege C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abolfazl","familyName":"Hashemi","name":"Hashemi, Abolfazl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Sharp Finite-Iteration Theory for Asynchronous Categorical Distributional Temporal-Difference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T19:07:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:08:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:18:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:54:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:34:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study finite-iteration behavior of asynchronous categorical distributional temporal-difference methods, covering scalar categorical TD (CTD) in the Cramér geometry and multivariate signed-categorical TD (MTD) in the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) geometry. We establish high-probability guarantees under i.i.d. sampling and under a continuing Markovian trajectory. For CTD, the Cramér sample complexity to the true return law has leading term $\\tilde O(ρ_{\\min}^{-1}(1-γ)^{-2}\\varepsilon^{-2})$ in the i.i.d. and $\\tilde O(μ_{\\min}^{-1}(1-γ)^{-2}\\varepsilon^{-2}+μ_{\\min}^{-1}t_{\\mathrm{mix}})$ in the Markovian setting, without using variance reduction or data dropping. For MTD, the MMD sample complexity has leading terms $\\tilde O(ρ_{\\min}^{-1}(1-γ)^{-1-c}\\varepsilon^{-2})$ and $\\tilde O(μ_{\\min}^{-1}(1-γ)^{-1-c}\\varepsilon^{-2}+μ_{\\min}^{-1}t_{\\mathrm{mix}})$, where $c\\in(0,2)$ is the homogeneity exponent of the MMD kernel, and they reduce to the CTD rates when $c=1$. These are, to our knowledge, the first finite-iteration rates for MTD. For undiscounted fixed-horizon policy evaluation, the same analysis applies to fixed-horizon versions of CTD and MTD under i.i.d. and episodic sampling, and the rates match the discounted ones with the effective horizon $(1-γ)^{-1}$ replaced by the horizon $H$ in the leading term. Matching minimax lower bounds show that the leading terms, and the implied $1$-Wasserstein rates, are optimal under i.i.d., Markovian, and episodic sampling. Together, these results provide a unified non-asymptotic analysis of asynchronous categorical distributional TD across scalar, multivariate, discounted, and fixed-horizon settings, with rates that are minimax optimal in their leading terms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"68 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06866","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:06:27Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:06:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:20:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoki","familyName":"Hiratani","name":"Hiratani, Naoki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit Target Shift in Online Learning: Characterization and Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T15:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:08:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-02T18:29:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:07:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online learning from a stream of data is a defining feature of intelligence, yet modern machine learning systems often struggle in this setting, especially under distributional shift. To understand its basic properties, we study the relationship between online and offline learning in the context of kernel regression by deriving a closed-form expression for the function learned by online kernel regression. We reveal that online kernel regression is equivalent to offline regression with shifted, inaccurate target outputs. Conversely, we show that by compensating for this implicit target shift in the teaching signal through target correction, online kernel-based learning can provably learn the same predictor as its offline counterpart. We derive both a closed-form expression for this target correction and an iterative form that can be applied sequentially. Applying this framework to continual image classification tasks on domain-incremental Split CIFAR-10 and CORe50, we show that online stochastic gradient descent with iteratively corrected targets outperforms learning with the true targets. This work therefore provides a basic framework for analyzing and improving online learning in non-stationary environments from the perspective of implicit target shift."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages; 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:30:40Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:30:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07782","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07782","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07782","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunhai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zunhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaofan","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Chaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiachong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiachong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CktFormalizer: Autoformalization of Natural Language into Circuit Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T14:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:03:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T15:19:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:21:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T03:24:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-04T11:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:21:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hardware infrastructure is a critical bottleneck for LLM-driven circuit design, limiting what agents can express, compile, and iteratively refine within an agentic loop. To address this bottleneck, we introduce CKTLEAN, a typed hardware infrastructure embedded in Lean. It supports hardware description, compilation to SystemVerilog, and interactive type-checking and proof feedback through a persistent read-eval-print loop (REPL). On this foundation, we build CKTFORMALIZER, an agent framework for hardware generation, repair, optimization, and source-level equivalence proving. We evaluate structural correctness through compilation, functional correctness through RTL and gate-level simulation, and formal correctness through proofs relative to stated specifications. Across VerilogEval, RTLLM, ResBench, and CVDP, CKTFORMALIZER with CKTLEAN achieves compilation rates of 91.1%-99.4%. Among designs that pass RTL simulation, 95.4%-100.0% jointly complete synthesis and place-and-route and pass design-rule and layout-versus-schematic checks. In a separate evaluation on 30 VerilogEval problems, interactive proof-state feedback raises kernel-accepted equivalence proof completion from 53.3% to 63.3%. Hardware evaluation feedback also guides architecture exploration and iterative power, performance, and area (PPA) optimization, with synthesis-area reductions of up to 58.6% in the optimization loop. These results suggest that typed representations and explicit proof-state feedback help structure the agentic loop: compiler diagnostics guide targeted repairs, while proof-state feedback helps agents identify what remains to be proved and determine the next proof step. Project Page: https://ckt-formalizer.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Tech Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07782","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:28:16Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:28:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07671","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07671","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07671","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lauri","familyName":"Lovén","name":"Lovén, Lauri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasu","familyName":"Tarkoma","name":"Tarkoma, Sasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Endogeneity of Miscalibration: Impossibility and Escape in Scored Reporting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.0; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"91B26, 91A40, 62C05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T12:42:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:57:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T16:28:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:02:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T12:00:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:20:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An agent's probability report is paid for twice: by a strictly proper scoring rule, and by an approval rule for the decision it triggers. In this classical decision-coupled setting, non-affine approval is known to defeat truthful reporting. We show the conflict is endogenous: when feasible, the welfare-maximizing approval rule is never affine. The distortion, however, is predictable and can be designed around. There is a reserve report at which pretending to be the marginal type costs exactly the approval prize. Approving at or above the reserve screens types perfectly under every strictly proper score, and the reserve does not depend on the type distribution. A Lipschitz rule with a single kink attains first-best exactly; under strict feasibility no continuously differentiable rule does. The binding constraint is steepness, not smoothness. First-best is attainable within a slope budget if and only if the budget is at least the critical slope: the steepest chord of the pretending cost up to the reserve. Below it the welfare loss is cubic in the shortfall. Where the pretending cost is convex up to the reserve, as for Brier, log and power scores, the critical slope is closed-form. The instances are AI-agent oversight and marketplace operation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages, no figures, all proofs inline"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07671","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:25:38Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07579","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07579","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07579","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunho","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yunho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwon","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Jongwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojin","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Woojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeonghoon","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Jeonghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Language Model is Its Own Critic: Reinforcement Learning with Value Estimation from Actor's Internal States"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T10:49:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T03:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:37:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T02:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:37:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-03T08:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:38:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) for Large Reasoning Models rests on variance reduction, which requires both a reliable baseline and high prompt diversity within each training batch. This is especially difficult in multi-domain training for general reasoning models, where prompts from different tasks induce highly diverse gradient signals. Existing approaches fall short in different ways: GRPO estimates its baseline as the group mean over rollouts from the same prompt, so an accurate baseline leaves fewer distinct prompts in the batch, while PPO avoids this trade-off by training a policy scale critic, roughly doubling the cost of training. We introduce POISE (Policy Optimization with Internal State Value Estimation), a reinforcement learning algorithm that turns the model's internal states into a value model. A lightweight probe reads the signals already computed during the forward pass to predict the baseline, and is trained online alongside the policy. To preserve gradient unbiasedness, we introduce a cross-rollout construction that predicts each rollout's value from an independent rollout's internal states. On Qwen3-4B and OLMo3-7B-Instruct-DPO across a six-domain verifiable-reward corpus, POISE outperforms other RLVR baselines while achieving more stable training. Moreover, the probe matches a separate LLM-scale value model, generalizes to various tasks, and remains accurate as the policy scales. By leveraging the model's internal representations, POISE enables stable policy optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2026; Project Page: https://holi-lab.github.io/POISE/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07579","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:23:19Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:23:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07280","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07280","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07280","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Muhammad","name":"Muhammad, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pasupuleti Dhruv","familyName":"Shivkant","name":"Shivkant, Pasupuleti Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak N.","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Deepak N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mask2Cause: Temporal Causal Discovery Beyond Causality in Mean"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T05:43:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-03T20:54:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One approach to discovering causal relationships in multivariate time series is to ask whether a variable's history improves prediction of another variable. When this improvement is measured solely by reductions in optimal squared error, the criterion misses relationships that affect the target's conditional variance without changing its conditional mean. We propose Mask2Cause, an end-to-end Transformer framework for causal discovery through conditional mean and variance prediction. Each variable's history is embedded as a separate token, and a single learnable adjacency matrix, shared across layers and attention heads, controls information exchange between tokens. Gaussian negative log-likelihood and a sparsity penalty on this matrix jointly train the forecasting model and graph. Our population analysis connects the optimal log-loss reduction to causally conditioned directed information, providing an information-theoretic motivation for the training objective. We introduce Mixed Physics, a benchmark with separately controlled mean and variance dependencies, to evaluate recovery of mean-only and variance-only edges. In its pure-variance setting, Mask2Cause achieves 1.000 off-diagonal AUROC, compared with 0.521 for a corresponding squared-error model. Experiments on linear, chaotic, biological, and real-data-derived benchmarks demonstrate competitive graph recovery, while the inferred graphs support downstream tasks such as causal pruning for efficient forecasting and root-cause localization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07280","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:16:15Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:16:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathn","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jonathn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arya","familyName":"Datla","name":"Datla, Arya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziv","familyName":"Goldfeld","name":"Goldfeld, Ziv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PLOT: Progressive Localization via Optimal Transport in Neural Causal Abstraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T21:52:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:17:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-04T23:10:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:43:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Causal abstraction offers a principled framework for mechanistic interpretability, aligning a high-level causal model with low-level neural computation through interchange intervention analysis. Finding such an alignment, however, often requires fitting and evaluating separate learned mappings across many candidate neural locations. We introduce PLOT, a gradient-free approach for joint correspondence discovery via a global matching of intervention effects. PLOT represents abstract and neural interventions by geometric signatures of their effects on the shared task output and fits an optimal transport coupling between the two collections. The coupling can be calibrated directly into an executable intervention handle or used progressively to select coarse parent sites for finer localization, reducing the cost of searching broad collections of neural sites. Experiments on hierarchical equality, binary addition, and multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) demonstrate fast and accurate direct handles without learning intervention rotations and show that progressive localization can improve accuracy under intervention-size constraints while reducing computational cost. PLOT can also guide gradient-based subspace learning, with PLOT-guided distributed alignment search (DAS) attaining accuracy comparable to full DAS at approximately $3.6\\times$ and $20\\times$ lower serial runtime on binary addition and MCQA, respectively. PLOT thus separates the search for candidate correspondences from optional subspace learning and provides a common framework for constructing and testing neural intervention handles for a given causal model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:09:07Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:09:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"TingFeng","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, TingFeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningxin","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Ningxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EnvSimBench: A Benchmark for Evaluating and Improving LLM-Based Environment Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T05:08:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:35:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T11:05:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:16:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scalable AI agents training relies on interactive environments that faithfully simulate the consequences of agent actions. Manually crafted environments are expensive to build, brittle to extend, and fundamentally limited in diversity. A promising direction is to replace manually crafted environments with LLM-simulated counterparts. However, this paradigm hinges on an unexamined core assumption: LLMs can accurately simulate environmental feedback. In practice, LLM-simulated environments suffer from hallucinations, logical inconsistencies, and silent state drift failures that corrupt agent reward signals and compound the construction costs that the paradigm was designed to eliminate. To address this gap, we propose EnvSimBench with four contributions: 1) We provide the first formal definition and operationalization of Environment Simulation Ability (EnvSim Ability) as a quantifiable research objective. 2) We construct EnvSimBench, a rigorous benchmark covering 400 samples across 167 diverse environments, equipped with verifiable labels and fine-grained difficulty stratification along three axes. 3) Systematic evaluations reveal that all state-of-the-art language models suffer from a universal state change cliff: they achieve near-perfect accuracy on tasks when the environment state remains invariant, yet fail catastrophically when multiple states need simultaneous updates. This finding exposes EnvSim Ability as a critical yet largely unaddressed capability gap. 4) We design a constraint-driven simulation pipeline that substantially reduces hallucination, boosts environment synthesis yield by 6.8%, and cuts costs by over 90%. Overall, EnvSimBench serves as both a diagnostic framework and a practical optimization path for reliable LLM-based environment simulation, establishing a foundation for scalable agent training. Code and data are available at https://github.com/cookieApril/EnvSimBench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:15:28Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.06943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.06943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil","familyName":"Sethi","name":"Sethi, Sahil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett","familyName":"Beaulieu-Jones","name":"Beaulieu-Jones, Brett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert L.","familyName":"Grossman","name":"Grossman, Robert L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProtoSSL: Self-Supervised Pretraining and Downstream Transfer for Projection-Based Prototype Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T21:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:14:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T15:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:37:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In domains where both predictive performance and interpretability are essential, deep neural networks achieve strong results but provide limited insight into how their predictions are made. Projection-based prototype networks address this limitation by grounding predictions in similarity to representative training examples, enabling case-based explanations and global prototype inspection. However, existing approaches rely on label supervision, tying prototypes to a specific task and requiring large labeled datasets. We introduce ProtoSSL, a framework for pretraining a foundational latent prototype bank on unlabeled data and transferring it to downstream tasks to create interpretable, projection-based prototype models. Our key idea is to separate motif discovery from label alignment. ProtoSSL first learns a transferrable prototype bank using a self-supervised objective applied directly to prototype activations, and then aligns these prototypes to downstream tasks through a novel efficient assignment procedure. Across six electrocardiography (ECG) datasets, ProtoSSL improves label efficiency, outperforming supervised prototype baselines in low-data regimes with as few as 256 labeled examples; with fine-tuning, ProtoSSL outperforms supervised prototype baselines at full dataset scale. In a human evaluation study, ProtoSSL produces prototypes and prototype-based explanations that are judged more favorably than those learned with direct label supervision. We further show that the framework extends to audio classification. Thus, ProtoSSL enables both learning foundational prototypes from unlabeled data before the downstream label space is known, and subsequent assignment to new tasks to create interpretable, projection-grounded prototypes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:08:16Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:08:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:50:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongqiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongxu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haijun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Offline Policy Optimization with Posterior Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T07:48:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:42:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-02T07:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:37:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A fundamental challenge in model-based offline reinforcement learning (RL) lies in the trade-off between generalization and robustness against exploitation errors in out-of-distribution (OOD) regions. The key to resolving this trade-off lies in enabling the model to explore OOD regions that remain consistent with underlying physical dynamics. However, achieving this is challenging because limited data cannot uniquely identify the dynamics model, and unconstrained exploration is risky. Existing methods often overlook this nuance, addressing the risk through excessive pessimistic regularization, which ensures robustness but sacrifices generalization. To address this, we propose PSPO, which treats the dynamics model as a random variable rather than a point estimate. This formulation inherently allows for controlled exploration of OOD regions. By alternately updating the posterior distribution and the policy, we design a regularized optimization algorithm with convergence guarantees. Experiments on standard benchmarks demonstrate that PSPO achieves superior performance compared to state-of-the-art baselines. Further analysis confirms that our method attains the desired pessimism-free property while maintaining robustness, and ablation studies verify the effectiveness of each proposed module."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:18:54Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:18:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07855","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07855","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07855","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin Wook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jin Wook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Szegda","name":"Szegda, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhisheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward L.","familyName":"Ionides","name":"Ionides, Edward L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jagged AI in Scientific Peer Review: Evidence from POMP Data Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T15:17:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T17:09:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T02:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T16:04:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-05T01:09:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their growing use in academic writing and statistical analysis, the performance of artificial intelligence (AI) tools in scientific peer review remains a largely unexplored area. A key challenge is jagged AI, a phenomenon where AI exhibits strong ability spikes in some domains while remaining deficient in others. To study this jaggedness in a practical data science context, we considered the task of reviewing partially observed Markov process (POMP) data analyses. POMP models, a generalization of state-space models or hidden Markov models, are used to fit mechanistic dynamic models to time series data in diverse applications including disease transmission, ecological dynamics, and financial risk assessment. High-quality peer review in this area entails assessment of scientific context, identification of errors in implementing complex algorithms, and decisions concerning methodological best practices. We studied 72 POMP projects from four semesters of a University of Michigan graduate time series course for which the project reports, the source code, and student peer reviews are anonymized and open access. We compared the human reviews with four AI review agents, using Claude Code with differing instructions implemented as skill files. We found that the AI review agents exhibited a jagged capability profile, identifying plausible technical errors and instances of invalid inference methodology overlooked by humans, while showing lower capability at finding issues involving interpretive errors, narrative coherence, and domain-informed model critique. The jaggedness was similar for all agents, consistent with other research finding that the specific instructions can have limited effect on the capability of AI models for some tasks. Skill file configuration shifted which weaknesses agents emphasized, without removing the jaggedness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07855","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:29:56Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:29:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07217","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07217","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07217","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sota","familyName":"Yoshihara","name":"Yoshihara, Sota","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamical Systems in Elliptical Pursuit and Evasion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"34C25 (Primary), 37C60, 37C27, 34D20, 37C25, 49N75 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T04:09:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T04:21:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:23:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T04:52:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:30:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the elliptical case of one-on-one pursuit and evasion problems. Using the simultaneous differential equations derived by Barton and Eliezer, we derive a dynamical system based on the assumption that the shape of the pursuer's trajectory is unaffected by the evader's speed. The dynamical system involves the angular difference between the velocity vectors of the players and their separation distance. We examine whether the pursuer lies inside or outside the evader's elliptical path, and how the angle between the directions of motion of the two players evolves. Moreover, using a complex variable that includes information about the logarithmic distance and the angular difference, we transform the system into a single ordinary differential equation along which the distance between any two solutions never increases. We obtain four main results. First, when the evader is slower than the pursuer, the pursuer captures the evader in finite time, and we derive an explicit upper bound for the capture time. Second, when the evader is faster than the pursuer, a pursuer starting inside the evader's elliptical path is never captured, whereas capture does occur from just outside it on a nearly head-on course. Third, in the same case, the system has a unique periodic solution, to which every solution that avoids capture converges. Fourth, this periodic solution is hyperbolic and exponentially stable, and the product of its Floquet multipliers is given explicitly."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v3: major revision. Theorem 3 of v2 (global convergence for n\u0026lt;1) is corrected: the periodic solution attracts every solution that avoids capture, but capture can occur for pursuers starting just outside the ellipse. The proofs are rewritten and extended. Sections 4 and 5 of v2 (simulation results) were published separately as S. Yoshihara, JSIAM Letters 18 (2026) 109-112, and are removed here"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07217","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:14:46Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:14:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:46:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Date","name":"Date, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y.","familyName":"Murakami","name":"Murakami, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Sekiguchi","name":"Sekiguchi, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Wada","name":"Wada, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Wakayama","name":"Wakayama, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The sigma meson ($f_0$) at finite temperature with truncated overlap fermions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T02:03:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:28:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T05:15:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:26:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T11:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:50:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the temperature dependence of meson screening masses in two-flavour lattice QCD using dynamical truncated overlap fermions, a type of lattice chiral fermions. The screening masses for the $π$, $ρ$, $a_1$, $a_0$, and the sigma $(f_0)$ mesons are extracted by computing spatial correlation functions. This exploratory study uses meson screening masses and the connected/disconnected decomposition of the scalar correlator as diagnostic observables to examine how the disconnected contribution changes across the pseudocritical region. Above the pseudocritical temperature $T_{\\rm pc}$, the $π$ and $f_0$ screening masses become degenerate, consistent with chiral restoration. The $(ρ,a_1)$ pair also shows the expected degeneracy. Decomposition of the $f_0$ propagator reveals that the connected contribution dominates above $T_{\\rm pc}$, while the disconnected part becomes significant below $T_{\\rm pc}$, explaining the reduced statistical clarity observed at low $T$. These results demonstrate that dynamical truncated overlap fermion simulations can capture the qualitative thermal behaviour of the scalar sector."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 13 figures and 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:12:34Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:12:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:46:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.08058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.08058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weam Abou","familyName":"Hamdan","name":"Hamdan, Weam Abou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chawakorn","familyName":"Maneerat","name":"Maneerat, Chawakorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Undulating Conformal Boundaries in 3D Gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T17:44:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:17:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T18:12:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:10:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider three-dimensional Einstein gravity in Euclidean signature with a finite boundary of torus topology endowed with an induced metric of fixed conformal class and a constant trace of extrinsic curvature $K$. For vanishing, positive, and negative cosmological constant $Λ$, we analytically determine boundaries enclosing different patches of locally flat, de Sitter (dS$_3$), and Anti-de Sitter (AdS$_3$) spaces. We find solutions that depend non-trivially on either cycle of the torus, noting that some of them exhibit self-intersections. Adapting the Gibbons-Hawking prescription of interpreting the Euclidean gravitational path integral as a thermal partition function, we explore the rich semi-classical thermodynamic phase space of the problem. While most saddles are found to be either thermally unstable or metastable compared to those with uniform boundaries, we find inhomogeneous solutions that are thermodynamically favourable in the case of $Λ\u0026lt; 0$ and $2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"64 pages + appendices, 9 figures; v2: new results added in section 5"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:34:37Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:34:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:32:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07745","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07745","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07745","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Oevermann","name":"Oevermann, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas D.","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Thomas D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The massive Thirring / sine-Gordon model with non-zero current density"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T13:51:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:27:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/vyq7-6hxx","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper determines the zero-temperature equation of state for the massive Thirring / sine-Gordon model. This demonstrates recently derived model-independent upper and lower bounds on the zero-temperature equation of state with fixed number density from systems with a non-zero current density. That approach is potentially valuable as Monte Carlo calculations with a current density avoid the sign problem in the Euclidean formulation. An advantage to illustrating these bounds in the massive Thirring / sine-Gordon model is that the relevant calculations with both a number density and a current density can be done using a Bethe ansatz. For this model, optimal bounds constrain the energy density as a function of number density by a factor of two from above and below at high densities for all choices of couplings. The lower bound becomes exact at low densities, while the upper bound approaches the worst constraint of a factor of 4.90."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages (5 pages main text, 3 pages appendix and references), (1+1) figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07745","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-05-11T02:27:26Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:27:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:32:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07563","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07563","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07563","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nacib G.","familyName":"Albuquerque","name":"Albuquerque, Nacib G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thiago R.","familyName":"Alves","name":"Alves, Thiago R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geraldo","familyName":"Botelho","name":"Botelho, Geraldo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinícius V.","familyName":"Fávaro","name":"Fávaro, Vinícius V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On hypercyclic and (common) upper frequently hypercyclic subspaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"47A16, 47B37","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T10:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:52:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T16:48:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:30:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $B$ be a unilateral weighted backward shift on $\\ell_p$, $1 \\leq p \u0026lt; \\infty$, that admits a $\\mathscr{U}$-frequently hypercyclic subspace. We prove that $B$ admits such a subspace free of frequently hypercyclic vectors. The proof technique we develop also allows us to prove that $B$ admits a hypercyclic subspace free of $\\mathscr{U}$-frequently hypercyclic vectors, and to solve a question posed by Bès and Menet in 2015 on the existence of common $\\mathscr{U}$-frequently hypercyclic subspaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The manuscript has been revised and polished in several places, and its title has been changed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07563","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:22:55Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:22:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:32:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07872","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07872","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07872","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuancheng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linli","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Linli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fandong","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video Understanding Reward Modeling: A Robust Benchmark and Performant Reward Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-08T15:29:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T12:14:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T01:19:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal reward models have advanced substantially in text and image domains, yet progress in video understanding reward modeling remains severely limited by the lack of robust evaluation benchmarks and high-quality preference data. To address this, we propose a unified framework spanning benchmark design, data construction, and reward model training. We introduce Video Understanding Reward Bench (VURB), a benchmark featuring 2,100 preference pairs with long chain-of-thought reasoning traces (averaging 1,143 tokens) and majority voting evaluation across general, long, and reasoning-oriented video tasks. We further construct Video Understanding Preference Dataset (VUP-35K) via a fully automated pipeline, providing large-scale high-quality supervision for video reward training. Building on the data, we train VideoDRM and VideoGRM, a discriminative and a generative reward model, both achieving state-of-the-art performance on VURB and VideoRewardBench. Further analysis confirms that VUP-35K enhances both reward performance and model reasoning capability, while VideoDRM and VideoGRM yield significant gains under best-of-$N$ test-time scaling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07872","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-11T02:30:21Z","registered":"2026-05-11T02:30:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:51:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07938","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.07938","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.07938","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sachini","familyName":"Weerasekara","name":"Weerasekara, Sachini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasha","familyName":"Darras","name":"Darras, Natasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagar","familyName":"Kamarthi","name":"Kamarthi, Sagar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Colles","familyName":"Price","name":"Price, Colles","affili